  "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{y}^{2}-4x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3{y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6,$ $x=3,$ and $y=2,$ and solve $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6(2))-(6)\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (12)-24 & =0 \\\\ 12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
